    "result_text": "All tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nIntroduced address normalization applied whenever an owner is created.\n\n**New file** \u2014 `AddressNormalizer.java` (mirrors the existing `TelephoneNormalizer` pattern): a `@Component` whose `normalize(...)` trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands whole-word abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). Whitespace-only / null input normalizes to `\"\"`.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 wired the normalizer in via the constructor and, in `addOwner`:\n- Normalizes the address, rejects it with **400** when blank after normalization, and stores/returns the normalized form (`owner.setAddress(...)`).\n- Household duplicate detection and the shared `householdId` now derive their `addressKey` from the normalized address on **both** sides of the comparison (new owner and each existing owner), so addresses are always compared in normalized form.\n\n**Test results:** the three new acceptance tests (`coreNormalizesWhitespaceAndCase`, `functionalityExpandsAbbreviations`, `errorRejectsBlankAfterNormalize`) and the full suite pass \u2014 `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nOne observation: the generated `OwnerFieldsDto.address` already carries a `Pattern` of `.*\\S.*`, so a whitespace-only address is also rejected at the bean-validation layer with 400 before reaching the controller; my explicit post-normalization blank check remains as the spec-required safeguard and keeps the rule enforced in the service logic itself.",
